\Babstract{
There should be a function template that returns the unsigned absolute of an integer,
as a UB-free counterpart to \tcode{std::abs}.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

C++ currently has a \tcode{std::abs} function that computes the absolute value of an integer.
This is sufficient for some but not all use cases
because it has undefined behavior for the minimum value representable in an integer type.

\Bug{
The following function template has undefined behavior when invoked with \tcode{INT_MIN}:
\cppblock{
template<class T>
struct sign_and_magnitude {
  bool sign;
  T magnitude;
};

template<class T>
sign_and_magnitude<T> decompose(auto x) {
  return { .sign = x < 0, .magnitude = std::abs(x) };
}
}

This happens because \tcode{std::abs} is implemented as \tcode{(x < 0 ? -x : x)},
and negating \tcode{INT_MIN} is UB due to the result not being representable in the result type.
}

WG14 has solved this problem with a
\ref("https://cstd.eisie.net/c2y.html#decl-uabs"){\tt{uabs} function in \tt{<stdlib.h>} in C2y},
which takes \tcode{int} and returns \tcode{unsigned int},
meaning that the result is always representable.
This was added by WG14 paper \ref("N3349").

The C++26 standard library has also solved this problem
with the exposition-only helper \tcode{\exposid{abs-if-needed}}
in \eelis{linalg.helpers.abs}.

\h2{Motivation}

C2y compatibility is part of the motivation,
but not the only factor.

Taking the absolute value of an integer is simply a fundamental operation
that any standard library should provide,
and while \tcode{std::abs} exists,
it has undefined behavior that makes it unsafe and unusable for certain use cases.
Consequently, \tcode{uabs} is a function that users frequently reinvent:
a GitHub code search for
\ref("https://github.com/search?q=language%3AC%2B%2B%20-is%3Afork%20%2F%5Cbuabs%5Cb%2F&type=code"){\tt{language:C++ -is:fork /\\buabs\\b/}}
yields 2.6K files at the time of writing.

The numbers for Rust's \tcode{unsigned_abs} are more compelling:
a GitHub code search for
\ref("https://github.com/search?q=language%3Arust+%2F%5Cbunsigned_abs%5Cb%2F+-is%3Afork&type=code"){\tt{language:rust /\\bunsigned_abs\\b/ -is:fork}}
yield 98K files at the time of writing.

\h2{Design}

\h3{Accepting wider integers}

The way C supports unsigned integers in \tcode{uabs} is problematic for adoption in C++
because it has a single overload:
\cppblock{
unsigned int uabs(int j);
}

This means that if C++ mirrored the overload set of \tcode{std::abs},
which also accepts \tcode{long} and \tcode{long long},
there could silently be different results because
argument truncation would take place only in C, but not in C++.
For example,
C's \tcode{uabs(1ll << 40)} produces zero due to truncation,
whereas \tcode{std::uabs(1ll << 40)} would produce \tcode{-(1ull << 40)}.

\Bnote{
\tcode{std::abs} has a similar issue:
\ul{
  \li{
    C's \tcode{abs(1ull << 40)} produces zero due to truncation,
    whereas \tcode{std::abs(1ull << 40)} produces \tcode{-(1ull << 40)}.
  }
  \li{
    C's \tcode{abs(1ull << 32)} may have UB due to negation of \tcode{INT_MIN},
    whereas \tcode{std::abs(1ull << 32)} produces \tcode{-(1ull << 32)}.
  }
}
}

However, \tcode{std::uabs} should still support these wider types
because it becomes unusable in generic code otherwise;
it could only be used with arguments of type \tcode{int} in particular.
Not accepting the wider types would also be surprising and inconsistent with the \tcode{std::abs}
overload set.

\h3{Accepting bit-precise integers}

There is no reason why \tcode{std::uabs} (or \tcode{std::abs}) couldn't accept
bit-precise integers.
The implementation is equally trivial for any kind of integer.

There is only a slight problem in that \tcode{std::abs} is not a template
but an overload set of concrete functions,
and it currently guarantees that the result is promoted.
That is, \tcode{std::abs} invoked with a \tcode{signed char} returns \tcode{int}.
Similarly, the C2y \tcode{uabs} function returns \tcode{unsigned int}.
This design shouldn't be extended to bit-precise integers
because it would result in a surprising change in category
from bit-precise integers to standard integers.

\h3{Accepting extended integers}

Extended integer types should also be accepted in both \tcode{std::abs} and \tcode{std::uabs}
because it would leave a strange gap if only standard and bit-precise integers were supported,
and this gap may be a problem for generic code that is intended to support any integer type.

For extended integer types,
it is possible to extend the existing overload-based design by requiring an additional overload
for any non-promotable extended integer type.
That is, there would be no overload for an \tcode{__int8} type,
but there would be an overload for a \tcode{__int128} type,
meaning that for a \tcode{__int8} input,
the overloads \q{\tcode{int std::abs(int)}} and \q{\tcode{unsigned std::uabs(int)}} are invoked.

It would also be possible for the template that supports bit-precise integer types
to accept extended integer types too.
However, that would be inconsistent with the fact that currently,
\tcode{std::abs} is designed to produce the result of \tcode{(j < 0 ? -j : j)}
\em{in type and in value}.
That is, when \tcode{-j} would be subject to integer promotion,
\tcode{std::abs} returns the promoted result too.
Furthermore, a template would not automatically accept unscoped enumeration types
whose underlying type is an extended integer type,
but those types are accepted by \tcode{std::abs}.

\Bnote{
Similarly, \tcode{std::to_chars}
mandates a separate overload for every standard or extended integer type,
so there is some precedent for a non-template design that accepts any integer type.
}

\h3{No overloads for unsigned types}

When calling C's \tcode{uabs} with \tcode{unsigned int},
there is an implicit conversion to \tcode{int},
and a negation takes place if the original value was greater than \tcode{INT_MAX}.
Therefore, accepting unsigned integers \q{directly} in \tcode{std::uabs}
(and returning the argument unchanged)
would silently yield different results in the two languages.

This is why,
even though it would be useful in generic code,
\tcode{std::uabs} should not accept unsigned integers directly.
Consequently, unsigned integers would fail overload resolution
since there would be several competing overloads for \tcode{int}, \tcode{long}.

\h3{No additional functions for specific types}

C2y also provides additional named overloads
such as \tcode{ulabs}, \tcode{umaxabs}, etc.
These are not proposed for C++ in this paper
because there is no particular urgency to add them;
they would exist purely for C compatibility.

It would be better to wait for the eventual rebase of the standard library on C2y,
especially considering that there have been recent changes to the names of these functions.
In particular, \ref("N3577") renamed \tcode{uimaxabs} to \tcode{umaxabs}.

\h2{Implementation}

A \tcode{std::uabs} overload set can be implemented as follows:

\cppblock{
template<class T>
std::make_unsigned_t<T> __uabs(T x) noexcept {
  return x < 0 ? -static_cast<make_unsigned_t<T>>(x)
               :  static_cast<make_unsigned_t<T>>(x);
}

// Standard integer types:
constexpr unsigned int           uabs(int x) noexcept { return __uabs(x); }
constexpr unsigned long int      uabs(long int x) noexcept { return __uabs(x); }
constexpr unsigned long long int uabs(long long int x) { return __uabs(x); }

// Extended integer types:
constexpr unsigned __int128      uabs(__int128 x) noexcept { return __uabs(x); }
// ...

// Bit-precise integer types:
template<\exposid{bit-precise-integer-type} T>
std::make_unsigned_t<T> uabs(T x) noexcept { return __uabs(x); }
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\Bimp{
The changes that introduce new overloads for bit-precise integer types
are only to be applied if \ref("P3666R5") (or later revision)
has been applied to the working draft.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

Add a feature-test macro to \eelis{version.syn} as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_uabs 20XXXXL // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<header>}}
}
}

In both \eelis{cstdlib.syn} and \eelis{cmath.syn},
change the synopsis as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
namespace std {
  \etc

  //\serif{ \eelis{c.math.abs}, absolute values}
  constexpr int abs(int j);                                            //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr long int abs(long int j);                                  //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr long long int abs(long long int j);                        //\serif{ freestanding}
\ins{  constexpr \exposid{extended-signed-type} abs(\exposid{extended-signed-type} j);          //\serif{ freestanding}
  template<class T> constexpr T abs(T j);                              //\serif{ freestanding}}
  constexpr \exposid{floating-point-type} abs(\exposid{floating-point-type} j);            //\serif{ freestanding-deleted}

  constexpr long int labs(long int j);                                  //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr long long int llabs(long long int j);                       //\serif{ freestanding}

\ins{  constexpr unsigned int uabs(int j);                                            //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr unsigned long int uabs(long int j);                                  //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr unsigned long long int uabs(long long int j);                        //\serif{ freestanding}
  constexpr make_unsigned_t<\exposid{extended-signed-type}> uabs(\exposid{extended-signed-type} j);  //\serif{ freestanding}
  template<class T> constexpr make_unsigned_t<T> uabs(T j);                      //\serif{ freestanding}}

  \etc
}
}
}

Change \eelis{c.math.abs} as follows:

\Bdiff{
¶
\note{
The headers \header{cstdlib} and \header{cmath}
declare the functions described in this subclause.
}

\ins{¶
For each function with a parameter of type \tcode{\exposid{extended-signed-type}},
the implementation provides an overload
for each extended signed integer type\iref{basic.fundamental}
that is not subject to integral promotion\iref{conv.prom},
where all uses of \tcode{\exposid{extended-signed-type}}
in the function signature
are replaced with that integer type.}

\itemdecl{
constexpr int abs(int j);
constexpr long int abs(long int j);
constexpr long long int abs(long long int j);
\ins{constexpr \exposid{extended-signed-type} abs(\exposid{extended-signed-type} j);
template<class T> constexpr T abs(T j);}
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\effects
\del{These functions have the semantics specified in the C standard library
for the functions \tcode{abs}, \tcode{labs}, and \tcode{llabs}, respectively.}
\ins{Equivalent to:
\tcode{return j < 0 ? -j : j;}}

\del{¶
\remarks
If \tcode{abs} is called with an argument of type \tcode{X}
for which \tcode{is_unsigned_v<X>} is \tcode{true} and if \tcode{X}
cannot be converted to \tcode{int} by integral promotion,
the program is ill-formed.}
\br\del{\note{
Allowing arguments that can be promoted to \tcode{int} provides compatibility with C.
}}
}

\itemdecl{
constexpr floating-point-type abs(floating-point-type x);
}

\itemdescr{
¶
\returns
The absolute value of \tcode{x}.
}

See also: ISO/IEC 9899:2024, 7.12.8.3, 7.24.7.1
}

Append the following items in \eelis{c.math.abs}:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
constexpr unsigned int uabs(int j) noexcept;
constexpr unsigned long int uabs(long int j) noexcept;
constexpr unsigned long long int uabs(long long int j) noexcept;
constexpr make_unsigned_t<\exposid{extended-signed-type}> uabs(\exposid{extended-signed-type} j) noexcept;
template<class T> constexpr make_unsigned_t<T> uabs(T j) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
¶
Let \tcode{U} be the return type.

¶
\constraints
\tcode{T} is a bit-precise signed integer type.

¶
\effects
Equivalent to:
\tcode{return j < 0 ? -static_cast<U>(j) : static_cast<U>(j);}
}
}

